Produced in the finished state, ready for use (or merely needing separation by cutting dividing threads) without sewing or other working (for example, certain dusters, towels.table cloths, scarf squares, blankets);

Cut to size and with at least one heat-sealed edge with a visibly tapered or compressed border and thc other edges treated as dcscribed in any other subparagraph of this Note, but cxcluding fabrics the cut edgcs of which have been prevcnted from unravclling by hot cutting or by other simple means;

Hemmed or with rolled edges, or with a knotted fringe at any of the edges, but excluding fabrics the cut edges of which have been prevented from unravelling by whipping or by other simple means;

(e)Cut to size and having undergone a process of drawn thread work;

()Assembled by sewing, gumming or othcrwise (other than picce goods consisting of two or more lengths of identical material joined end to end and piece goods composed of two or more textiles assembled in layers, whether or not padded);

(g)Knitted or crocheted to shape, whether presented as separate items or in the form of a number of items in the length.



8.For the purposes of Chapters 50 to 60:

(a)Chapters 50 to 55 and 60 and, except where the context otherwise requires, Chapters 56 to 59 do not apply to goods made up within the meaning of Note 7 above; and 

(b)Chapters 50 to 55 and 60 do not apply to goods of Chapters 56 to 59.

9.The woven fabrics of Chapters 50 to 55 include fabrics consisting of layers of parallel textile yarns superimposed on each other at acute or right angles. These lavers are bonded at the intersections of the yarns by an adhesive or by thermal bonding.



10.Elastic products consisting of textile materials combined with rubber threads are classified in this Section.



11.For the purposcs of this Scction, the expression "impregnated" includes "dipped".

12.For the purposcs of this Scction, thc cxpression "polyamides" includes "aramids".

13.For the purposes of this Section and, where applicable, throughout the Nomenclature, the expression "elastomeric yarn" means filament yarn, including monofilament, of synthetic textile material, other than textured varn, which does not break on being extended to three times its original length and which returns, after being extended to twice its original length, within a period of five minutes, to a length not greater than one and a half times its original length.

